summary:
The most interesting part about OpenAI getting a gold medal in the IMO competition is not really the result itself but rather how it was achieved. Two things were interesting:
1) OpenAI are deliberately prioritising methods that are general rather than specific;
2) They have made progress on hard to verify tasks.
The second point is particularly key. So far RL proved to work on clear maths problems, but there was a bit of a question mark whether RL would be useful in other areas that don't have a precise clear answer. It looks like the answer is 'yes'. The implication of this is that we shouldn't hit an RL wall very quickly, once we run out of clear maths problems to train on.
@alexwei_ (OpenAI Researcher): "Previously a lot of RL was more focused on what you can do when you have verifiable rewards. We were seeing more improvement on these harder-to-verify tasks, and that’s what made us excited."
@polynoamial (OpenAI Researcher): "The techniques we used - for scaling up thinking time, for working on hard-to-verify tasks, and for parallel compute - are all general-purpose methods that we’re either planning to use or have already used in other systems as well."
